  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nAnother round of light showers is moving into central South Dakota \nearly this morning. These rain showers will linger over central and \nnorth central South Dakota through the morning and early afternoon \nbefore progressing eastward. Expectations are that these showers \nwill then dissipate this evening into the early overnight over \neastern South Dakota. In general, rain rates will remain fairly \nlight, and only a couple hundredths of accumulation is expected at \nthe most. Another round comes in on Wednesday night into Thursday as \nan upper-level low passes to the south. The Aberdeen forecast area \nwill be on the very northern edge of the precipitation from this \nsystem, meaning that rain will mainly be concentrated in central \nSouth Dakota. This time a bit more accumulation may be in store for \nareas that see rain, but still only about a 20-50% chance to see a \ntenth of an inch over central South Dakota. \n\nWith near-normal temperatures in place for the next few days, \novernight temperatures will flirt with the freezing mark. Early \nWednesday morning, temperatures are forecast to fall just below \nfreezing over portions of north central and northeastern South \nDakota, although there are still some questions left to be answered \nas to exactly how cold it will get. Specifically, whether or not \nlows get to freezing will depend on how cloud cover clears out \novernight. The latest REFS guidance does show a decent signal for \nsome clearing over north central South Dakota, so confidence is \nhighest over that area at this time. Further east clouds will likely \nhang on a bit longer, which will limit the potential to reach 32 \ndegrees. Given the remaining uncertainty, will opt to hold off on \nany frost or freeze headlines at this time. Thursday morning will be \nslightly warmer, in no small part due to cloud cover expected, \nlimiting radiational cooling. However, Friday is expected to clear \nout and once again see portions of the forecast area drop below \nfreezing. Chances to freeze will again be highest along the North \nDakota/South Dakota border (~40-70%), but nearly the entire forecast \narea has a 10% chance or greater to see low below 32 degrees. \n\nPressure gradient will tighten Thursday behind a low pressure center \npassing to the north. Strong northwesterly winds will develop as a \nresult. The latest NBM probabilities to reach Wind Advisory criteria \n(wind gusts greater than 45 miles per hour) peak at 30-50% over \nparts of central and north central South Dakota, so can't rule out \nthe potential for an Advisory. These wind speeds can often be \nunderdone under northwesterly flow, although the cold air advection \ncomponent of that bias is not quite as strong as past situations \nwhere the NBM has significantly underestimated the wind gusts. \nRegardless of exact strength, the strong winds will be enough to \npush Grassland Fire Danger to High over central and north central \nSouth Dakota Thursday afternoon. \n\nBy Friday and into the weekend, and upper-level ridge will build \ninto the region. Both 500mb heights and 850mb temperatures (~5820dm \nand 15-18 degrees Celsius respectively) will be over the 90th \npercentile for mid-October, setting the stage for above normal \ntemperatures to return to the forecast area. While the ridge is in \nplace there will be limited chances for precipitation, so expect a \ndry weekend.",
